#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130353 :tutorial/content "Rotation-scaling matrices are involved in an important theorem regarding complex eigenvalues.\n\nTo sketch out the key idea, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/06/06a28b9b9d8db65a4214ed1d446c68f288978acc59afd3720873da7f825efe13.svg)\n\nwhich has the complex eigenvalue \n\n$$\n\\lambda=1-2\\textrm{i}={\\color{blue}1}+({\\color{red}-2})\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding complex eigenvector \n\n![](images/1b/1b7aa45404b3e6e02cb9802da371f6c74f6848f4b5601261f2257e12b7d362d0.svg)\n\nNow, we construct a rotation-scaling matrix $C$ using the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ as follows:\n![](images/0f/0f29362b05dd18b6a8a307f531cea6d59c083301703285bde41a9488f228c386.svg)\nWe also construct a matrix $P$ whose columns are the real and imaginary parts of the corresponding eigenvector:\n![](images/d1/d1b2efc5b22e34c0bb4cb93a4777c8d8139ca0047253b7177bd1809a272f5b37.svg)\n\nThe **rotation-scaling theorem** states that $A$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\nA=PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is true in this case:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PC{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d79a993f-cb2c-5ec9-a24b-ddc7bd94485d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3594 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130354 :tutorial/content "Let's now formally state the rotation-scaling theorem:\n\n*Let $A$ be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix with real entries. If $\\lambda$ is a complex (not real) eigenvalue of $A,$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda,$ then *\n\n$$\nA = PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n*where*\n![](images/74/74b63ce33cfa7779a6668a22974b709d549843a221c3b13067887e8193c6e6f0.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When two matrices $A$ and $C$ are related via the equation $A = PCP^{-1},$ we say that they are **similar.**\n\n- The rotation-scaling theorem tells us that every $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ real matrix with complex (non-real) eigenvalues is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We call the right-hand side of $A = PCP^{-1}$ the **rotation-scaling form** of $A.$ We can also refer to this as the **rotation-scaling decomposition** of $A.$\n\n- The matrix $P$ *must* be constructed from the eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue defined by $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09a26114-9068-50b4-897e-76504d4f7e68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3595 :tutorial/title "The Rotation-Scaling Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130355 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/74/74c17c58d6b39ff6a13ec56e135ec17ce0f260d2e9d90e562544637f6e677db0.svg)\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n![](images/15/1542ac2d3f79e1494ecf9e67dda3219966efdf76267ebda82d7689555dc4df30.svg)\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n![](images/f0/f0d562472e2b230a0cd393829ab4a5dec380e6fd1e2a797af4f1f651aae88f75.svg)\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e77afe5-c30a-56bd-95a6-037f1a903eda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3596 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of Rotation-Scaling Form"}]}
